ay be relevant to some of  you is you know we recognize it it's a  real trick to go from you know growing  in a very small scale and then wanting  to really actually change your practices  and move into something at a larger  scale or more commercial scale so we do  have some advanced enterprise online  courses that are designed for supporting  farm diversification well the mushroom  one is one for example but we have a  high tunnel classes starting shortly we  have both vegetable and berry production  classes that are online to give people  to start or deepen their skill sets  around those two production systems we  have a grazing course all of these are  meant to sort of help with the scale up  and we're going to be adding food safety  and even adapting to climate change so  these are some of the courses that were  adding that are really meant to help  deal with scale up and but we are  current poor funding supports also we  have funding from the USDA to help those  individuals who maybe three to five  years in and want to re strategize and  what we're actually doing is helping  farms create what we call profit teams  and these are teams of consultants often  extension educators who they hire to  help them improve their own  entrepreneurship business training and  planning and help them make decisions  really important decisions to grow the  business and so we have funding each of  the farms who participate get two  thousand dollars as a grant and then  they have to commit 500 of their own  funds so they have twenty-five hundred  dollars to work with over 18 months to  pay people to consult with whether  that's a feed a person to help them with  their nutrition plan if it's someone to  help them with their disease management  plan if it's about how do i improve the  quality for a wholesale market it's up  to the farmer what we want to know is  that how they really feel and all we  care is that they find the right people  to help them take the business to the  next level so this is very much  one-on-one consult  and then the other thing we're doing is  developing these sort of intensive  training programs that will be on  scaling equipment this is something  that's really tricky to go from a very  small scale even to move from sort of  less than and a half an acre to 1 acre  25 acres and then to 10 like there's  tend to be breaks they're like well  actually at over five acres and then  over 25 acres I think there's like some  natural breaks in scale that suggest a  scaling up of equipment and what do you  pick and how do you pick it and what's  going to be most adaptable going forward  so our training programs will focus on  scaling up equipment and then the  wholesale market readiness which we  think diversifying markets is really  important as we move into this and as we  see this growth in local foods the last  thing I just want to talk about and in  share with you is this a whole area of  work that we're doing right now focused  on military veterans and its really  trying to create pathways for veterans  to find employment on farms or to  actually start farms so I wanted to draw  your attention to it because I think  that all of you are really out there and  interfacing with a lot of folks and we  are getting more veterans returning from  service that we are trying to help them  integrate back into community and  whether it's agriculture our gardening  or farming we know there's really  meaningful benefits to them to be  involved with this kind of physical work  so i am going to just I can share more  with you but I don't I think i want to  have enough time for conversation but we  have a whole new area of work that's  developing around supporting veterans  and in this August we're going to host a  one-week intensive that's called armed  to farm for military veterans and it  will take place in central New York this  year  was western New York last year at  Central New York this year and then next  year will be in eastern New York so if  you feel people you know people who  might be interested please let us know  and pay attention to our announcements  when they come out so as I start to  close I want to make sure you know the  folks that can help you in the program  we have a really awesome staff at the  small farm program a violent stone who  leads our wholesale market stuff and  general inquiries Erica who's provided  leadership to the beginning farmer  project and online courses Ryan maría he  has facilitated the educator network and  also is really focusing on soil health  management and creating appropriate  technologies for small farms Matt Weiss  he's new to the program and he's leading  our current beginning farmer grant but  also shepherding the veterans work Steve  Gabriel who many of you might know is  that his background and a graffiti wrote  the book forest farming with Ken Mudge  and he's now joined our program and he's  provide he's being the managing editor  of the small farm quarterly and Cathy  pack who is brand new to the program but  is helping us dig more aggressively into  how do we help cultivate local food  systems and networks for farmers to  really grow their businesses in this  area so I think that's really all I  wanted to share with you which is sort  of the program what we're up to and  well you can always reach out to me or  to any of the staff at the program and  just send us an email if you're looking  for something and you can't find it  this has been a production of Cornell  University on the web at cornell edu


